<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nakhon Ratchasima</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Korat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nakhon Ratchasima, commonly called Korat, is a city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and the capital of the country's largest province, on the south-western edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorat-plateau">Khorat Plateau</a>. Founded as a fortified Siamese frontier town in the 17th century, it was the gateway to the Lao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> lands of the Isan region. A monument in the city honours Thao Suranari, heroine of the resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anouvong">Anouvong</a> of Vientiane in 1826.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nakhon-ratchasima.jpg" alt="Nakhon Ratchasima" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khaosaming · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thao_Suranaree_Statue_Korat_Thailand.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nakhon Ratchasima lies about 250 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, at the western edge of the Korat Plateau. It is the largest city of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and one of the four major cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorat-plateau">Isan</a>. Historically it marked the boundary between Lao and Siamese territory. Its name joins Nakhon Raj, from the ancient town of Khorakapura, with Sema, another ancient town; both lay in Sung Noen District, 32 kilometres to the west. The short name Korat comes from the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> name Angkor Raj.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the 14th century the area was under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> suzerainty, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phimai">Phimai</a> to the north was probably more important. In the 17th century King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narai">Narai</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> ordered a new city built on the site as a stronghold on the north-eastern frontier, and Siamese chronicles called it a second-class city ruled by a hereditary royal governor. After the Burmese destroyed Ayutthaya in 1767, a son of King Boromakot tried to rule from Phimai over Korat and other eastern provinces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">King Taksin</a> of Thonburi sent the brothers Thong Duang and Boonma, and the prince was executed in 1768. Thong Duang later became King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a>.</p><p>In 1826 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anouvong">Chao Anouvong</a> of Vientiane, thinking Siam weakened, attacked Korat in a rebellion against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a> that lasted two years. Lady Mo, wife of the deputy governor, is credited with freeing the city from his army, and a statue in the centre of the city honours her. The railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> reached Korat in 1900 and was opened on 21 December 1900 by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a>. In October 1933 the city was the headquarters of the Boworadet Rebellion against the new government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nakhon Ratchasima is the capital of the province of the same name and the third-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. Its economy has long rested on agriculture, as a processing centre for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, tapioca and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorat-plateau">Isan</a>. From 1962 to 1976 Korat Royal Thai Air Force Base hosted Thai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> air force units during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nakhon Ratchasima” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The regime controlled the north-eastern plateau around Phimai and <b>Nakhon Ratchasima</b>, a region with old Khmer temples and Lao and Khmer populations.</blockquote><a href="/state/phimai-regime">Phimai regime</a></li></ul></section>
